  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 113 PM CDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nDry conditions are expected through tomorrow with increased high \nlevel cloudiness after midnight through sunrise. The only exception \nto this will be across west central Kansas where an isolated \nthunderstorm will be possible late this afternoon into early this \nevening. All other activity should remain well north of the area. An \nupper level shortwave will then move through the area tomorrow night \nincreasing lift across the area. Cloudiness will increase in \ncoverage tomorrow afternoon with mostly cloudy skies overnight. Some \nthunderstorms will develop across eastern Colorado tomorrow \nafternoon and sweep across the forecast area overnight. Any \nlingering storms will be confined to central Kansas Friday morning. \nWinds through this period will be from the southeast through \ntomorrow morning, then shift to the south for the remainder of the \nday. As for temperatures, highs this afternoon will generally be in \nthe 90s with around 100 degrees across far western Kansas. Lows \ntonight are expected to range from the mid 60s north to low 70s \nalong the KS/OK border and far southwestern Kansas. Highs tomorrow \nwill range from the low 90s along the I-70 corridor to low 100s \nacross far southwestern Kansas. Lows tomorrow night are expected to \nrange from the mid 60s across west central Kansas to mid 70s across \nsouth central Kansas. \n\nThe remainder of the forecast looks dry as a strong upper level high \nanchors itself over the Central Rockies and Central Plains. Well \nabove normal temperatures are expected during this period. Highs \nwill reach into the 90s Friday with upper 90s to low 100s expected \nfor the remainder of the forecast. Chances are high that heat \nheadlines will need to be issued this weekend into early next week. \nLows will generally be in the upper 60s to upper 70s.",
